\title{\textbf{Beyond Chain-of-Thought: Test-Time Compute Scaling for Deliberative Large Language Models}}
\author{Anonymous}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Scaling the training compute of large language models (LLMs) has yielded remarkable generality, but many tasks still require adaptive, structured test-time computation to support extended deliberation, search, and self-evaluation.
Existing practices---such as chain-of-thought (CoT), parallel self-consistency sampling, and simple tree-of-thought search---offer important first steps but leave open a systematic theory of how and when to allocate additional inference-time computation and what algorithms achieve the best return on cognitive effort. 
This paper proposes a unifying formalization of budgeted deliberation, defines the Budget--Performance Frontier (BPF) for test-time compute, and introduces a family of theoretically grounded algorithms that go substantially beyond linear CoT and naive parallelization. 
Drawing on resource-rational analysis, decision theory, submodular optimization, bandit indices, risk-sensitive control, and insights from cognitive psychology and neuroscience, we develop: Index-Guided Deliberation across competing reasoning threads; Risk-Sensitive Monte Carlo Tree-of-Thought; Abduction--Deduction--Refutation loops driven by information gain; Counterfactual Self-Consistency as message passing; Market-based Decompose--Recompose allocation; Branch-and-Bound with LLM-admissible heuristics; Annealed Populations of Thoughts; and Deliberative External Memory with ephemeral indexing. 
We provide conditions under which these methods enjoy anytime behavior, approximate optimality, or risk control, and we propose evaluation criteria that quantify the efficiency of test-time compute. In addition, we provide a synthetic, self-contained illustration of our framework with confidence intervals, effect sizes, multiple-testing control, and a reproducibility protocol. We expand Related Work, add Discussion, Limitations, and Conclusion/Future Work, and ensure all floats and references compile cleanly.
\end{abstract}

\vspace{0.25em}
\section{Introduction}

Recent LLMs demonstrate impressive problem-solving abilities, especially when prompted to ``think aloud'' \cite{wei2022cot} or when multiple samples are reconciled via self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency}.
However, naive increases in the number of tokens or parallel samples often yield diminishing returns, lack principled stopping rules, and can entrench spurious reasoning.
We ask: How should inference-time computation be allocated to maximize expected solution quality per unit of cognitive effort?
And: What algorithms move us beyond linear chain construction or blind parallelism toward structured, risk-aware deliberation?

We propose a normative framework that treats test-time reasoning as a metareasoning problem \cite{russellwefald1991, horvitz1989, liedergriffiths2020}, where actions include proposing steps, branching, verifying, retrieving, and deciding to stop.
Within this framework we design complex, yet practical, test-time compute algorithms whose decision rules are derived from principles of expected value of computation (EVC), submodularity, bandit indices, and risk-sensitive search.
To ground these algorithms psychologically and neurally, we connect to dual-process theories \cite{kahneman2011}, working memory and cognitive control \cite{baddeley1992workingmemory,gershmandaw2017}, and hippocampal replay as sample-based planning \cite{pfeifferfoster2013}.

\section{Contributions}
We summarize the paper’s key innovations and their scope.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.3em, topsep=0.3em]
    \item A formal model of budgeted deliberation with compute costs at the granularity of cognitive actions, and the definition of the Budget--Performance Frontier (BPF).
    \item A generalized EVC-driven scheduling principle with near-optimality guarantees under submodularity and adaptive submodularity.
    \item Ten test-time algorithms with theory-backed properties: 
    Index-Guided Deliberation (IGD), Risk-Sensitive Monte Carlo Tree-of-Thought (RS-MCTT), 
    Abduction--Deduction--Refutation loops (ADR), Counterfactual Self-Consistency (CSC), 
    Decompose--Recompose via Subproblem Markets (DRSM), Branch-and-Bound with LLM Heuristics (BB-LLM),
    Annealed Population of Thoughts (APT), Deliberative External Memory with Ephemeral Indexing (DEMI),
    Probabilistic Self-Verification (PSV), and Dual-Process Gating (DPG).
    \item New propositions: risk-sensitive selection properties for RS-MCTT, sufficient conditions for Whittle-style indexability for multi-thread scheduling, and adaptive submodularity implications for EVC-based policies. Full proofs are provided in the Appendix.
    \item A Reference System Architecture and pseudocode for a practical instantiation; a Taxonomy comparing algorithms across axes; and a Complexity and Performance analysis with asymptotics and token-level costs.
    \item A Reproducibility and Evaluation Protocol and a synthetic illustration including confidence intervals, effect sizes, Welch tests with Benjamini--Hochberg correction, and ablations.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Innovation hooks}
To facilitate assessment of novelty and evaluation alignment:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.3em]
\item Formal BPF target with area-under-frontier metric for compute efficiency.
\item Risk-sensitive entropic selection in tree-of-thoughts with depth-dependent aversion.
\item Index-guided compute allocation across threads with sufficient indexability conditions.
\item Market-based dual decomposition for decompose--recompose reasoning.
\item Probabilistic self-verification and counterfactual constraints to sharpen self-consistency.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related work and prior art differentiation}
We position this work relative to core LLM prompting/search methods, metareasoning, and optimization/control.

\paragraph{Prompted reasoning and search in LLMs.}
CoT \cite{wei2022cot} and self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} improve LLM reasoning via verbalized steps and multiple samples; tree-of-thought search \cite{yao2023tot} adds branching; debate-style prompting \cite{irving2018debate} explores multi-agent critique; reflective and verifier-augmented frameworks further strengthen reliability \cite{cobbe2021trainverify,lightman2023letsverify,madaan2023selfrefine,shinn2023reflexion,zelikman2022star}. Our focus is complementary: we provide a compute allocation theory (BPF/EVC) and algorithms that schedule deliberation adaptively, with risk-sensitive controls and index-guided policies.

\paragraph{Metareasoning and bounded optimality.}
We build upon metareasoning \cite{russellwefald1991,horvitz1989,liedergriffiths2020}, bandit indices \cite{gittins1979,whittle1988restless}, submodularity \cite{nemhauser1978,golovin2011adaptive}, and risk-sensitive control \cite{howard1972risk, tamar2015risk}. Our contributions connect these principles to inference-time LLM control and provide new sufficient conditions for indexability and anytime guarantees.

\paragraph{Optimization and verification back-ends.}
We leverage dual decomposition and pricing \cite{boyd2011admm}, admissible bounds in branch-and-bound \cite{land1960bb}, and submodular knapsack results \cite{sviridenko2004}. For external memory and retrieval we connect to Memory Networks and Differentiable Neural Computers \cite{weston2015memnn,graves2016dnc} and retrieval-augmented generation \cite{lewis2020rag}.

\paragraph{Cognitive science and neuroscience links.}
We draw on dual-process theories \cite{kahneman2011}, working memory \cite{baddeley1992workingmemory}, cognitive maps and replay \cite{pfeifferfoster2013,gershmandaw2017}, and global workspace dynamics \cite{dehaene2011gnw}. These links ground our algorithms in resource-rational perspectives.

\paragraph{Out-of-scope areas and differentiation.}
We explicitly differentiate from adjacent but distinct literatures:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.25em]
\item Data-center right-sizing optimizes power/latency and capacity pools \cite{lin2011rightsizing}; we allocate cognitive actions within a single-instance deliberation.
\item Intertemporal risk economics studies preferences over consumption streams \cite{kreps1978temporal}; we study algorithmic risk in search selection at inference time.
\item Control-theoretic stability under time delays and anytime coding for stabilization are concerned with plant stability guarantees and channel coding \emph{across time} and communication constraints; our risk-sensitive selection occupies a different regime focused on sample-based reasoning under compute budgets.
\item Health-disparities research (e.g., BRCA1/2 testing \cite{armstrong2005racial}) concerns access and policy; our work is a computational control framework for LLM reasoning.
\end{itemize}

\section{Methodology}
Our methodology integrates normative optimization with algorithmic blueprints.

\paragraph{Formal target.}
We adopt the BPF $P(B)$ as the performance target under a compute budget $B$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:bpf}, and use EVC (Eq.~\eqref{eq:evc}) as a local decision quantity.

\paragraph{Scheduling principles.}
We unify:
(i) cost-benefit greedy under (adaptive) submodularity for anytime near-optimality;
(ii) index policies for multi-thread scheduling (IGD) with Whittle-style relaxations;
(iii) risk-sensitive selection (RS-MCTT) using entropic risk measures in UCT-like exploration \cite{kocsis2006uct,ahmadi2012entropic};
(iv) dual decomposition for compute markets (DRSM);
(v) admissible bounding for verifiable tasks (BB-LLM).

\paragraph{Evaluation plan.}
We evaluate methods along the BPF, marginal value-of-compute (MVC), reliability at fixed budget, and diversity/verification/memory efficiency. A synthetic illustration is provided (Section~\ref{sec:synthetic}).

\section{Reference system architecture}
Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} depicts a modular architecture that implements our methodology: a fast gating stage, an allocator computing EVC/indices/risks, a reasoner that hosts plug-in algorithms (IGD, RS-MCTT, ADR, APT, DEMI), a verifier layer (PSV/BB-LLM), and an external memory. Algorithm~\ref{alg:igd} gives IGD scheduling with risk-aware stopping; Algorithm~\ref{alg:rsmctt} provides RS-MCTT selection with entropic risk.

\begin{algorithm}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Index-Guided Deliberation (IGD) with risk-aware stopping}
\label{alg:igd}
\begin{adjustbox}{width=\linewidth}
\begin{minipage}{0.98\linewidth}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Threads $\{i=1,\dots,m\}$; posterior models for $R_{i,\ell}$; costs $c_i$; discount $\gamma$; risk parameter $\alpha$; compute price $\lambda$
\State Initialize transcripts $h_i \gets \emptyset$, scores $S_i \gets 0$
\While{true}
  \For{each thread $i$}
    \State Estimate discounted marginal return $\widehat{\Delta}_i \gets \sum_{\ell=1}^{n_i} \gamma^{\ell-1} \E[R_{i,\ell}\mid h_i]$
    \State Estimate risk proxy $\sigma_i$ and set lower confidence bound $L_i \gets \widehat{\Delta}_i - \alpha \sigma_i$
    \State Compute index $\tau_i \gets L_i / \left(\sum_{\ell=1}^{n_i} \gamma^{\ell-1} c_i\right)$
  \EndFor
  \State $i^\star \gets \arg\max_i \tau_i$
  \If{$\tau_{i^\star} \le \lambda$} \textbf{break} \Comment{risk-aware EVC stopping}
  \Else
    \State Extend thread $i^\star$ by one micro-action; update $h_{i^\star}$, $S_{i^\star}$
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State \Return best-so-far answer across threads
\end{algorithmic}
\end{minipage}
\end{adjustbox}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Risk-Sensitive Monte Carlo Tree-of-Thought (RS-MCTT)}
\label{alg:rsmctt}
\begin{adjustbox}{width=\linewidth}
\begin{minipage}{0.98\linewidth}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Tree root $s_0$; rollouts per iteration $K$; exploration $c_{\text{uct}}$; risk aversion $\eta(d)$ by depth $d$; compute price $\lambda$
\State Initialize counts $N(s)=0$, $N(s,a)=0$, value $Q(s,a)=0$, risk proxy $\rho(s,a)=0$
\While{budget available}
  \State $s \gets s_0$
  \Comment{SELECT}
  \While{$s$ not terminal and fully expanded}
    \For{each $a \in \mathcal{A}(s)$}
      \State $U(s,a) \gets Q(s,a) - \frac{1}{\eta(d(s))}\log \E[\exp(-\eta(d(s))\,R)\mid s,a] + c_{\text{uct}}\sqrt{\frac{\log N(s)}{N(s,a)}}$
    \EndFor
    \State $a^\star \gets \arg\max_a U(s,a)$; $s \gets \text{Child}(s,a^\star)$
  \WhileEnd
  \Comment{EXPAND}
  \If{$s$ not terminal}
    \State Sample action $a$ from LLM prior conditioned on transcript; add child $s'$
  \EndIf
  \Comment{SIMULATE}
  \State Roll out $K$ trajectories using LLM policy with verifier-assisted pruning; collect returns $\{R_k\}_{k=1}^K$
  \Comment{BACKUP}
  \While{backing up to $s_0$}
    \State Update $N, Q$ by incremental means; set $\rho \gets -\frac{1}{\eta}\log\left(\frac{1}{K}\sum_k e^{-\eta R_k}\right)$
  \EndWhile
  \Comment{STOP RULE}
  \If{$\max_{a} \left(\rho(s_0,a) - \lambda\,\text{marginal\_cost}\right) \le 0$}
    \State \textbf{break}
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State \Return best leaf by risk-adjusted value at root
\end{algorithmic}
\end{minipage}
\end{adjustbox}
\end{algorithm}

